{"name":"Green Stain","hex":"#2b553e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green stain","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of green rot (“wood disease”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green_stain"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stain","blocks":[{"text":"A stain is a discoloration that can be clearly distinguished from the surface, material, or medium it is found upon. They are caused by the chemical or physical interaction of two dissimilar materials. Accidental staining may make materials appear used, degraded or permanently unclean. Intentional staining is used in biochemical research, and for artistic effect, such as in wood staining, rust staining and stained glass."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Patternspage.jpg/330px-Patternspage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stain","wikidata":"Q2733470"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Waistcoat%2C_naval_%28AM_2015.38.1.3-5%29.jpg/500px-Waistcoat%2C_naval_%28AM_2015.38.1.3-5%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Waistcoat%2C_naval_%28AM_2015.38.1.3-5%29.jpg/1280px-Waistcoat%2C_naval_%28AM_2015.38.1.3-5%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Waistcoat%2C_naval_%28AM_2015.38.1.3-5%29.jpg/500px-Waistcoat%2C_naval_%28AM_2015.38.1.3-5%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stained waistcoat, originally that of a Royal Navy captain of the Victorian era","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWaistcoat%2C_naval_(AM_2015.38.1.3-5).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Br%C4%85zowe_plamy_ubt.jpeg/500px-Br%C4%85zowe_plamy_ubt.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Br%C4%85zowe_plamy_ubt.jpeg/500px-Br%C4%85zowe_plamy_ubt.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coffee stains","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABr%C4%85zowe_plamy_ubt.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jeweller